      "description": "Composed well over 2500 years ago, Odysseus' long and perilous journey home after the fall of Troy is quite simply one of the greatest stories ever told. Filled with monsters, gods, and legendary lands, The Odyssey blurs the veil between myth and reality. Yet behind its fantastical yarn-spinning of witches and cannibal giants lies the real world of the Late Bronze Age Mediterranean. Shaped by ancient kingdoms, genuine seafaring cultures, and groundbreaking archaeological discoveries. \n\nWith the new Nolan movie coming out today, I just had to investigate the real story behind the myth. And what a story it is!! \n\nWhat's your favourite depiction of the legend? Did you enjoy the new Nolan movie? Are you going to see it? Let me know what you think in the comments...\n\nHistory Time is written, narrated, edited, produced by @PeteKellyHistory \n\nJoin myself, Dakota of Earth & History With Kayleigh in Anatolia this September !\nhttps://www.thecommunitygarden.org/tours/p/ancientanatolia\n\n---- The Real Odyssey ---\n7:00 - Part 1: Homecoming\n30:31- Part 2: Finding Ithaca\n48:20 - Part 3 - Real Odyssey \n\n--- The Real Odyssey Bibliography ---\nEmannuel, Jeffrey P, Black Ships & Sea Raiders , 2017\nBoardman, John. The Archaeology of Nostalgia: How the Greeks Re-Created Their Mythical Past., 2002.\nBurkert, Walter. Greek Religion. Translated by John Raffan,, 1985.\nFinley, M. I. The World of Odysseus. Revised ed., 2002.\nHomer. The Odyssey. Translated by Emily Wilson, , 2018.\nLatacz, Joachim. Troy and Homer: Towards a Solution of an Old Mystery. , 2004.\nLord, Albert B. The Singer of Tales. Harvard University Press, 1960.\nMorris, Ian, and Barry Powell, editors. A New Companion to Homer. Brill, 1997.\nPowell, Barry B. Classical Myth. 2020.\nSnodgrass, Anthony M. Archaeology and the Rise of the Greek State\nWood, Michael. In Search of the Trojan War., 1998.\n& many more \n\n— Become a patron for as little as a dollar a month & help keep this channel